ns-xwidget-scroll: move xwidget views when ns_scroll_run copies rows.

NS (Cocoa) only.  When redisplay scrolls a window by copying rows
(scroll_run_hook) instead of drawing them again, a copied row's glyphs
are not drawn, so x_draw_xwidget_glyph_string does not run for an xwidget
in it and the view stays where it was last drawn.  On NS that left every
xwidget view behind by the height of each line scrolled: under one-line
scroll-up-command, wheel and pixel-precision scrolling, and a page scroll
that keeps the widget on screen, for WebKit and native views alike.
force-window-update did not move the view; redraw-frame did.

x_scroll_run has a HAVE_XWIDGETS branch for this case and ns_scroll_run
had none.  This ports it: each XvWindow subview of the frame's EmacsView
whose widget lies in the run's rows is moved by the run's displacement
and reclipped to its text area with the formula of
x_draw_xwidget_glyph_string, so a later draw of the same glyph finds
nothing to move.  It differs from the X branch in two ways:

- A view is in the run when the widget's whole rectangle overlaps the
  run's rows, where X tests the view's visible part against the band
  actually copied.  A widget lies inside its glyph row, so both select
  the rows' widgets, but the X test misses a widget clipped to nothing
  before the scroll and one in rows the copy leaves out because they lie
  outside the text area (a partly visible row under pixel scrolling);
  those move with their rows all the same.
- A view clipped to nothing is sized to zero height, as the NS draw path
  does, instead of hidden; xwidget_end_redisplay still hides a view
  whose glyph left the matrix.

A view whose xwidget was killed is skipped: on NS nsxwidget_kill leaves
it in the EmacsView with its model set to nil.  Touches only
src/nsterm.m, in the ns_scroll_run that fix-ns-scroll-crash changes.

diff --git a/src/nsterm.m b/src/nsterm.m
index 943c1a8..ba7e06a 100644
--- a/src/nsterm.m
+++ b/src/nsterm.m
@@ -2964,6 +2964,52 @@ ns_scroll_run (struct window *w, struct run *run)
 
   gui_clear_cursor (w);
 
+#ifdef HAVE_XWIDGETS
+  /* Move the xwidget views whose glyphs are in the copied rows, as
+     x_scroll_run does: redisplay does not draw a copied row again, so
+     nothing else moves its views.  A widget lies inside its glyph row,
+     so it is in the run when it overlaps the run's rows, whether or not
+     it is visible; the run's rows include those the copy above leaves
+     out because they lie outside the text area.  */
+  {
+    int run_y = WINDOW_TO_FRAME_PIXEL_Y (w, run->current_y);
+
+    for (NSView *subview in [FRAME_NS_VIEW (f) subviews])
+      {
+	if (![subview isKindOfClass: [XvWindow class]])
+	  continue;
+
+	struct xwidget_view *xv = ((XvWindow *) subview).xv;
+
+	/* nsxwidget_kill leaves the view of a killed xwidget in place,
+	   with no model.  */
+	if (xv->hidden || !XWIDGETP (xv->model) || XWINDOW (xv->w) != w)
+	  continue;
+
+	int widget_height = XXWIDGET (xv->model)->height;
+
+	if (xv->y >= run_y + run->height || xv->y + widget_height <= run_y)
+	  continue;
+
+	int text_area_x, text_area_y, text_area_width, text_area_height;
+	window_box (w, xv->area, &text_area_x, &text_area_y,
+		    &text_area_width, &text_area_height);
+
+	xv->y += run->desired_y - run->current_y;
+	xv->clip_top = max (0, text_area_y - xv->y);
+	xv->clip_bottom = max (xv->clip_top,
+			       min (widget_height,
+				    text_area_y + text_area_height - xv->y));
+
+	nsxwidget_move_view (xv, xv->x + xv->clip_left,
+			     xv->y + xv->clip_top);
+	nsxwidget_resize_view (xv, xv->clip_right - xv->clip_left,
+			       xv->clip_bottom - xv->clip_top);
+	nsxwidget_move_widget_in_view (xv, -xv->clip_left, -xv->clip_top);
+      }
+  }
+#endif
+
   {
     NSRect srcRect = NSMakeRect (x, from_y, width, height);
     NSPoint dest = NSMakePoint (x, to_y);
